 pushing  policy in local governments they are  putting their own actual bodies in front  of um  kind of people who are destruction  destructing um our world um and there  are people in workplaces in homes in  schools who are also leading this and i  think that sometimes we get a bit  distracted by those in the forefront and  sometimes we actually rely on them to do  a lot of the heavy lifting without  realizing that we could also be climate  leaders and take up that position as  well richard do you think that's right  that actually some of the real heroes  are people we've never heard of  yeah i think there's an awful lot of  real heroes that we never we we we hear  of i was thinking of some of the uk  diplomats for example who've been  involved in cop 26 who worked all the  hours that were you know there were on  this earth over there over the last year  and probably their names aren't known  very much outside their own lunch times  as the  as the um as the saying goes i think  basically you know you've got three  constituencies that are vitally  important here one's the public one is  politicians and government and the other  is business and you can find examples of  real leadership uh in all of them just  take one example electric cars why are  we now going to a situation where  motoring is going to be electric we've  had scientific and technical innovations  we've had groups like greenpeace who are  ahead of the time arguing for these  things we've had a norwegian government  that grasped the metal grassed the  nether story and went ahead and  implemented it and showed it could be  done and we've had business leaders like  elon musk who have really changed the  entire business and investment  conversation about electric cars so it's  the whole mix there so joyce would you  agree with that you're nodding away  there we should give credit to people  across the board  yes i think we need to realize that you  know climate change and the climate  action and actually any social action  that's happened in the past although  there seemed to be um these kind of  figureheads or people at the um helm of  the um of the auction it's actually a  really huge group effort and there are  so many different perspectives that come  in so many different parts of that  action that have huge domino effects on  what actually ends up happening in the  end um and we need to really keep that  in mind otherwise you know we we do  those people a disservice who have put  so much hard work into bringing these  together and we reduce the problems that  we have we reduce the complexity and the  amount of different perspectives and  skills that we need to fix our issues  okay well joycelyn london richard black  always fascinating to get your views  thanks very much indeed for joining us  well tomorrow i'll be joined by  environmental activist judy lin wong and  editor-in-chief of business green james  murray so join us for that but for today  thanks for watching see you next time  [Music]  you

