oth of you  nice to see you both  so first of all the government is to  invest a hundred million pounds into  sizewell c a nuclear power station in  suffolk that will likely cost more than  20 billion pounds in total and take  nearly a decade to build so an important  vote of confidence in a low carbon power  source or would taxpayer money be better  spent elsewhere richard what do you  think do we need nuclear power has the  latest crisis over gas supply and rising  energy prices demonstrated a need for  for another source of homegrown energy  do you think  for it matters terribly much either way  to be honest um as tom was saying  earlier tom clark um you know there are  a lot of analysts who say that basically  britain would be better off with a  certain amount of nuclear power but you  have to remember that neither this nor  hinckley is going to come online for at  least a decade and so by then i suspect  that the energy system will look rather  different renewables will be even  cheaper than they are now i think  storage will be much cheaper than it is  now and those are much quicker and  easier to build as well so one thing i  think the government has been wise about  though is if it is going to build if it  does want nuclear power stations to be  built if it views these as a kind of  national asset then involve public money  because the costs of financing it are  just lower if you put the some of the  public welly behind it and that's one of  the reasons why this is projected to be  a little cheaper than the hinckley deal  well yes and monies always comes up in  the conversation doesn't it joycelyn but  what about this idea of of needing a low  carbon energy source that's reliable  whatever the weather i mean nuclear  fulfills that doesn't it what do you  make of it as an option  yeah of course um from a science  perspective nuclear is really important  because um currently we're in a position  to have a really stable renewable grid  um but i think i'm gonna echo what uh  richard just said about when this is  going to come online it's really not  part of our net zero uh target the the  timeline for them and also the expense  that needs to go into them they're more  of a legacy project um so i think we  need to be really intentional about  where we're putting a huge amount of our  focus and our priority into building  infrastructure and could that be going  into making a more reliable grid with  renewables as they are considering how  much quicker they have decreased in  price and increased increase in  reliability um nuclear has been around  for a very long time and we're still not  really getting uh the results that we  need um for this  low carbon grid um so those would be my  reservations yes and yet richard it's  really interesting to look at other  countries and their take on nuclear i  mean france obviously backs it  germany in the meantime is phasing out  its entire nuclear capacity isn't it and  partly over safety fears i mean it was  triggered after the fukushima um  disaster wasn't it that their  decision-making so what do you make of  what's going on in elsewhere in europe  well the german decision was actually  made first in the year 2000 and then  shortly before fukushima entirely  coincidentally the government decided to  elongate the lifetime of nuclear power  stations and then when we had fukushima  they reinstated the original date and  basically all the german nuclear  stations will be closed at the end of  this year really the german situation is  all to do with the cold war germany was  the frontline state in europe for a  possible nuclear conflict during the  cold war and that's  that's left and  that's left a definite imprint on the  german psyche and no one really in the  country is up for nuclear at all which  obviously from a climate change point of  view isn't great but i think given the  history of germany it's entirely  uh understandable there aren't many  countries in the world that plan to go  big on nuclear you look at china you  look at india but even then their  nuclear ambitions are dwarfed by their  renewable ambitions and as we saw china  you know installed a vast amount of  offshore wind um uh last year and then  you've got a few of the countries like  france as you as you said but really on  a global scale one expects the share of  nuclear electricity to be dwindling and  that's for a whole number of reasons but  i think number one is the cost and the  ease of building renewables a very quick  thought from you jocelyn on this i mean  are you saying basically you just like  the money all spent on renewables  um it's not that the money should all be  spent on renewables you have to remember  that the climate crisis isn't just about  energy and it's not a sort of one-issue  crisis we obviously have to spend money  in lots of different places but it's  just prioritizing how much of that money  and because the money in it kind of  shows the public what the uh focus is  for the government and so it should be  proportional to what is really working  and what is really going to get us to  the targets to ensure that customers and  the public really understand what is uh  what has efficacy and what works and  what is going to get us to our targets  okay let's move on to our next topic and  we're asking who are the role models  that we look up to on climate action  today and we're asking this because of  the sad news this week of the death of  sir crispin takel at the age of 91. that  he was a diplomat an environmentalist  who had the ear of margaret thatcher and  wrote a book in 1977 called climactic  change and world affairs which argued  for an international treaty on pollution  and richard i understand that you did  know crispin to kell so tell us a little  bit about him and how influential you  think he was in changing minds on  climate policy  yes i spent a bit of time with him in  the last decade basically when we opened  the energy and climate intelligence unit  um in 2014 so crispin was one of a  number of people who came onto our  advisory board um and he was he was  extremely interesting guy very urbane  very diplomatic in that sense of word  had a wry sense of humor but his impact  both on the uk and internationally i  think is far greater than his public  profile as as you said you know he  convinced margaret thatcher basically  that climate change was a serious issue  in 1989 she devoted her entire annual  speech to the u.n general assembly to  the environmental crisis and it's a  fantastic speech i would urge anyone to  go and read it now margaret thatcher was  a scientist herself and one of the  things that crispin said to me was that  when they talked about any other issue  she  was a politician when they talked about  climate change she acted as a scientist  so basically without that the history of  the uk uh involvement the cross-party  consensus the involvement of the  conservative party in climate change  would have been very different and if  she had not called for a un treaty in  that speech in 1989 perhaps we wouldn't  have a united nations climate convention  so joyce it's really interesting isn't  it that there are figures like this that  seem to have had a big impact would you  agree that it it's generally speaking  government advisors and government  ministers who have the most impact  because they're changing policy or do  you think we should give a shout out to  celebrities or influences that start  shifting public opinion which puts  pressure on politicians who would you  say are the best most effective role  models at the moment  i think i would have to disagree with  both i think we're missing a huge trick  in understanding that climate leadership  and climate impact is not equivalent to  status and that value doesn't come with  visibility and there are lots of climate  leaders  who don't have any status who aren't  known at all who are really making huge  changes um either they are pushing  policy in local governments they are  putting their own actual bodies in front  of um  kind of people who are destruction  destructing um our world um and there  are people in workplaces in homes in  schools who are also leading this and i  think that sometimes we get a bit  distracted by those in the forefront and  sometimes we actually rely on them to do  a lot of the heavy lifting without  realizing that we could also be climate  leaders and take up that position as  well richard do you think that's right  that actually some of the real heroes  are people we've never heard of  yeah i think there's an awful lot of  real heroes that we never we we we hear  of i was thinking of some of the uk  diplomats for example who've been  involved in cop 26 who worked all the  hours that were you know there were on  this earth over there over the last year  and probably their names aren't known  very much outside their own lunch times  as the  as the um as the saying goes i think  basically you know you've got three  constituencies that are vitally  important here one's the public one is  politicians and government and the other  is business and you can find examples of  real leadership uh in all of them just  take one example electric cars why are  we now going to a situation where  motoring is going to be electric we've  had scientific and technical innovations  we've had groups like greenpeace who are  ahead of the time arguing for these  things we've had a norwegian government  that grasped the metal grassed the  nether story and went ahead and  implemented it and showed it could be  done and we've had business leaders like  elon musk who have really changed the  entire business and investment  conversation about electric cars so it's  the whole mix there so joyce would you  agree with that you're nodding away  there we should give credit to people  across the board  yes i think we need to realize that you  know climate change and the climate  action and actually any social action  that's happened in the past although  there seemed to be um these kind of  figureheads or people at the um helm of  the um of the auction it's actually a  really huge group effort and there are  so many different perspectives that come  in so many different parts of that  action that have huge domino effects on  what actually ends up happening in the  end um and we need to really keep that  in mind otherwise you know we we do  those people a disservice who have put  so much hard work into bringing these  together and we reduce the problems that  we have we reduce the complexity and the  amount of different perspectives and  skills that we need to fix our issues  okay well joycelyn london richard black  always fascinating to get your views  thanks very much indeed for joining us  well tomorrow i'll be joined by  environmental activist judy lin wong and  editor-in-chief of business green james  murray so join us for that but for today  thanks for watching see you next time  [Music]  you
 

